Pipeline Operations Explained
1. Regex Column Standardization
The pipeline processes raw headers by stripping edge spaces, converting upper-to-lower transition points into underscores (converting TenureMonths into tenure_months), translating non-alphanumeric characters into single underscores, and lowering all characters. This produces standardized headers compatible with SQL tables and analytical toolings.  
2. Modern Categorical Selection
Rather than relying on legacy dtype checks, the workflow filters categorical fields using explicit dtype targets (object and string). Each string variable is converted into Pandas dedicated string type and stripped of accidental spaces (e.g., converting "Male " to "Male").  
3. Numerical Type Enforcement
To protect downstream numeric calculations from string-corrupted numbers, numerical variables are converted explicitly. Integer fields (age, tenure_months, support_tickets) and floating-point monetary fields (monthly_charges, total_charges) are cast with strict error handling to flag non-numeric values.  
4. Floating-Point Mathematical Auditing
Data entry errors or system bugs can cause total charges to drift from actual billed history. The pipeline calculates the expected lifetime billing using monthly charges multiplied by tenure months, rounded to two decimal places. It then applies absolute tolerance evaluation to confirm equality while accounting for floating-point binary representation limits.  
5. Data Quality Audit Log
Before saving, the pipeline aggregates all validation outcomes into an audit summary. This provides immediate verification of total rows processed, column count, null counts, duplicate records, customer ID uniqueness, and total charge mathematical integrity.  
